n/id #uuid "c1ebae7a-831a-5e25-819a-240d6378a505" :question/math-academy-id "q-25253" :question/problem "If the expression $y^{2} - 8y + 12$ is written as $(y + p)^{2} + q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71551962-dc06-573b-8d45-b362af81bab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141615" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-12x+28.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+28\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $28$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8e66e0-1fdd-51a2-8016-4e40bb8a0e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269653} {:answer/id #uuid "630bc6a6-f3ae-5fd4-a731-cbd31fbffa42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-9{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270144} {:answer/id #uuid "715bac65-d0bb-5e25-8584-22a34c234373" :