{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d85fc46-850a-5c84-b5f9-35452de37bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321370} {:answer/id #uuid "44269fc8-5ddc-5684-9a2c-f2df92134197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321684 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186576407}]} {:answer/id #uuid "94c0a1e3-7755-553c-8ed6-117195d88b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322330} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be16ea-f2f9-542f-96e8-bd3c135b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322803} {:answer/id #uuid "fccccf58-378d-5bfc-9fa6-35c13dd2fd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44269fc8-5ddc-5684-9a2c-f2df92134197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321684} :answer-field/id #uuid "701aa2ec-89fe-5586-b37e-c29f85e40b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "682d7daf-19ef-5f29-895c-817178660f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-3667" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+12x+35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6{)}^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6{)}^{2}-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bc4897-6558-52b5-bb24-657fa38a810c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400932} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6fb47-ecea-56a8-9070-83fc55e7254f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400933} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeaea36-9352-52ca-8483-6b276a77d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400935} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc66d56-1a8c-5626-b30a-e9774929570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bbc964-13ea-55a0-9065-a7c49f46fb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd1c73ef-6818-5b51-baee-cca065c2f0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141539" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d53fe67-7a37-5135-9bda-59dc26da7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400939} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c7488-0854-5209-9e72-78867a6a1be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400940} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec775e-a603-50a4-a821-b02cc8bdf4a2" :answer/type {