nt of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+87\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${10}^{2},$ writing the constant term $87$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{10}^{2}+87\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+10{)}^{2}-{10}^{2}+87\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+10{)}^{2}-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=10$ and $q=-13.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fd8789-93ec-5954-83e2-cf23dd67a277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=12,\\,q=272" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511780} {:answer/id #uuid "3481349c-cc15-5b93-8bee-1a7a74073821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=12,\\,q=116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511783} {:answer/id #uuid "40dc3e60-1945-57d5-919d-f4d2c0aa6cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=12,\\,q=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511785} {:answer/id #uuid "7081a2e8-1ce4-5ab0-87fb-f817c995fd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=24,\\,q=-448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511797} {:answer/id #uuid "c4497e49-b8fc-5569-b439-a5801a1be674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=12,\\,q=128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40dc3e60-1945-57d5-919d-f4d2c0aa6cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=12,\\,q=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511785} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e0409a7-21c1-5e39-8f65-6a86cb